# S02 — DECISIONS (append-only)

Format: `- date · question · choice · reason · who ruled`. Never edited, never deleted, only
appended. **Before any seat asks V a question, it checks this file** — a question already
answered here is re-asked to nobody.

## Inherited: intake dispositions and V-row defaults that bind S02

- 2026-09-06 · Who writes requirements, plans, and per-cluster reviews? · All three are Opus 5 subagents in fresh sessions; Grok 4.6 judges each finished UI element · V named the coders and the finished-element reviewer and nobody else · orchestrator (intake), pending V-1
- 2026-09-06 · V's wording "a tickbox that states 'I agree with the privacy policy'" vs the design's 8a sentence · The design's wording, verbatim: `I agree to the Privacy Policy, including that my debates may be published publicly.` · V deferred to the design in the same sentence ("idk how its written in the design") · orchestrator (intake C1), resolved
- 2026-09-06 · V's "I agree" button vs the design's `I have read it`, and the caption "must reach the end" · Label is the design's `I have read it`; the button stays disabled until the policy has been scrolled to its end marker · The label is resolved by the design; the scroll behaviour is the artboard's own caption and V did not mention it · orchestrator (intake C2), row V-2 default
- 2026-09-06 · V says clicking the tick opens the modal; the design makes the words "Privacy Policy" the link · Both entry points open it. Clicking the UNCHECKED box or the link opens 10c without ticking; the box is ticked ONLY by `I have read it`; `×`, Esc and backdrop leave it unchecked; a CHECKED box unchecks directly with no modal · Neither source is wrong; they describe two affordances onto one behaviour · orchestrator (intake C3), resolved
- 2026-09-06 · The design's modal footer has `Download PDF`; the repo has no policy PDF and nothing generates one · The button is NOT rendered; the footer keeps the contact line and `I have read it` · A download that fabricates a file is dishonest (standing V law) · orchestrator (intake C4), row V-3 default
- 2026-09-06 · A GDPR consent gate with no server-side record of the acceptance or the policy version · UI gate only; the registration request keeps its exact shape; a follow-up ticket proposes `privacy_accepted_at` + policy version server-side · V's goal is front-end only; the contract and the security zone are out of scope for this mission · orchestrator (intake C6), row V-5 default
- 2026-09-06 · The live 18+ label `I affirm that I am at least 18 years old.` vs the design's `I am 18 or over.` in a bordered custom group · Adopt the design's group and wording; the backend field `adult_affirmed` is unchanged · 8a is the design of record for the sign-up card · orchestrator (intake C7), row V-6 default
- 2026-09-06 · `Privacy notice` in S01's 10b opens 10c, which S02 builds · Not a contradiction; S02 builds the modal FIRST as a standalone prop-driven component whose read-only mode has no consent side effect · Sequencing belongs to architecture · orchestrator (intake C8)

## REQ-01's own decisions

### The checkbox group

- 2026-09-06 · What is the new input called? · `privacy-accepted`; `adult-affirmed` is kept unchanged · Matches the existing hyphenated form-name convention, and keeping `adult-affirmed` preserves both the FormData read at `SignUpFlow.tsx:72` and the source guard at `authRoutes.source-test.mjs:45` · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · How does a click on the privacy row's label text avoid toggling the input natively? · The handler calls `preventDefault()` on the open-the-modal path, and the SPEC pins all three entry points (square, text, link) as separate test cases · A `<label>`-wrapped input toggles on any label click; without `preventDefault` the box would tick at the same moment the modal opens, which is exactly the behaviour V forbade. This is the single most likely implementation slip in the slice · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Custom 17px check squares, or native checkboxes restyled? · A real `<input type="checkbox">` stays in the DOM — focusable, `Space`-toggleable, labelled — with the 17px square rendered as a styled sibling or pseudo-element · Replacing the input with a `<div role="checkbox">` would lose `required`, native form semantics and the source guard's regex, for no visual gain · REQ-01

### The scroll gate

- 2026-09-06 · What mechanically counts as "reached the end"? · `container.scrollTop + container.clientHeight >= container.scrollHeight - 8`, evaluated at mount, on `scroll`, and on `resize` · Synchronous and pinnable: a jsdom test defines the three properties and dispatches `scroll`. **Rejected:** an `IntersectionObserver` on the end marker — jsdom implements none, so every pin would need a stub with asynchronous callback timing, which is a flake factory · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Why 8px of slack rather than exact equality? · The body text is 11.5px at line-height 1.65 ≈ 19px per line, so 8px is under half a line — the end cannot be counted as reached while a line of policy is still hidden — while still absorbing sub-pixel and browser-zoom rounding, which exact equality does not · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Does the gate re-close if the reader scrolls back up? · No; it latches on first satisfaction · Otherwise a reader who reaches the end and scrolls back to re-read section 3 finds the button disabled again, which is hostile and is not what "you were shown the whole text" means · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · A policy short enough to need no scrolling · Enables the button at mount · `scrollHeight <= clientHeight` satisfies the criterion for free; no special case is written · REQ-01

### The modal

- 2026-09-06 · Modal size, which the design gives only as a fixed artboard · `width: min(680px, calc(100vw - 32px))`, `max-height: 92vh` · 680 is the artboard's own modal width (760 less the 40px inset each side); 92vh approximates the artboard's vertical proportion (592 of 660 = 89.7%). Derived from the design, not invented · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · The eight jump pills and the eleven sections share no naming scheme · Mapping fixed in S02-R11: CONTROLLER→05, WHAT WE COLLECT→01, LAWFUL BASIS→06, PUBLISHING→03, MODELS & TRANSFERS→04, RETENTION→07, YOUR GDPR RIGHTS→08, COMPLAINTS→11. Sections 02, 09 and 10 get no pill · Each pill matches exactly one section by content; the design ships 8 pills for 11 sections deliberately, so no pill is invented for the remainder · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · The pill order is not document order (CONTROLLER is section 05 but listed first) · Keep the design's order verbatim; do not sort · A coder's reflex is to sort them into document order; the design's order is the design · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Section element ids · `policy-section-01` … `policy-section-11`, each pill carrying `data-jump="<id>"` · Makes the mapping mechanically checkable in jsdom even though the scroll itself is not · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · How is pill scrolling verified, given jsdom has no layout? · Split honestly: jsdom pins the MAPPING (eight pills in order, each target id present); V pins the SCROLL at acceptance step 12 · Claiming a jsdom test covers scrolling would be a fabricated verification · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · What does the footer show in `mode="read"`, which the design never depicts? · The contact line plus a single `Close` button; no `I have read it` · The design only ever shows 10c "opened from sign-up", so it has no read-only presentation to copy. A button reading `I have read it` that consents to nothing would be a UI claiming a thing it does not do. `Close` is the one string this mission adds beyond the design — routed as contested row Q7-04 · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Initial focus in the modal · The `×` close button · It is the first control in DOM order and gives a keyboard user an immediate exit; focus then moves naturally into the pills and the scroll region · REQ-01

### Gating, data and boundaries

- 2026-09-06 · Where does the gate live — the `required` attributes, the disabled button, or the handler? · All three; the handler refusal is the load-bearing one · A bare `form.dispatchEvent(new Event("submit"))` bypasses HTML constraint validation, and that is exactly how the existing suite submits (`auth-flow-integration.test.tsx:41-48`), so `required` alone gates nothing against a scripted submit · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Do the checkbox states need React state, or can FormData be read at submit? · React state for both · The button's `disabled` attribute must reflect the boxes live, with no submit — that is observable and is what V's acceptance steps 2-4 test · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Where does the policy text live? · Two exported constants in `apps/ui/lib/privacyPolicy.ts`; the component holds no prose · Forced by a live guard: `authRoutes.source-test.mjs:48` bans `terms` and `privacy notice` (case-insensitive) in `SignUpFlow.tsx`. Keeping the prose out of the component file too makes byte-exactness a single-file review · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · Does S02 add CSS tokens? · No. It consumes `--scrim`, `--z-policy-scrim`, `--z-policy-card`, `--ok-edge` and `--ok-dot`, all declared by S01 · The token test asserts exact set equality between declarations and map keys, so a second writer would conflict on every addition · REQ-01
- 2026-09-06 · The policy asserts retention periods, a sub-processor URL, a controller entity and a version date that the repository cannot confirm · Recorded as UNVERIFIED in the SPEC, not as defects; no seat re-derives them · They are business facts and the codebase is not their source of truth. Checked and TRUE: the contact domain (`TopBar.tsx:31`), the 18+ affirmation (`SignUpFlow.tsx:186`), mandatory MFA (`settings/page.tsx:49`) · REQ-01

### Rework round 1 — REQ-REV-01's verdict, applied (2026-09-06; `SPEC.md` is now v2, `SPEC-v1.md` is the archive)

- 2026-09-06 · Controlled or uncontrolled checkboxes, given the button must reflect them live? (REQ-REV-01 **B2**) · **Uncontrolled `<input type="checkbox">` + an `onChange` mirror into React state used ONLY for the submit button's `disabled`; `FormData` stays the truth at submit.** S02-R17 rewritten, S02-R18 now names `FormData` as the value it tests, §Tests gains the idiom rule · v1 said the component "holds both in React state rather than reading them only from FormData", i.e. controlled — and the reviewer's standalone jsdom + React probe, running this repo's own idiom (`field(x).checked = true`, `auth-flow-integration.test.tsx:324,448,466`) and its own submit helper (`:41-48`), three runs each: **controlled → `register()` 0 calls** (the suite goes 14/17), **uncontrolled + mirror → 1 call**. Assigning `.checked` fires no React `change`, so a controlled input's state stays false and R18 correctly refuses. Re-verified by me at `SignUpFlow.tsx:186` (no `checked`/`onChange` prop) and `:63,:72` (FormData read) · REQ-01 rework R1, mechanism from REQ-REV-01 B2
- 2026-09-06 · Rejected alternative for B2, recorded so nobody re-derives it · Making the inputs controlled and rewriting the three existing cases to click the boxes · It works, but it edits three passing cases' mechanics rather than their data, enlarges S02's diff inside a shared auth suite, and buys nothing: the button's live `disabled` is satisfied by the mirror alone · REQ-01 rework R1
- 2026-09-06 · How must a NEW test assert the submit button's enabled state? (REQ-REV-01 B2, second defect) · By dispatching a real `change`/click inside `act` — `field(name).click()` or `dispatchEvent(new Event("change", { bubbles: true }))` — **never** by assigning `.checked`. Assignment stays correct for the three existing SUBMIT cases and for R18's refusal pins, which exercise the handler and `FormData`, not the button · v1's own R17 hook ("enabled with both") could not be satisfied by the idiom used three times in the very file it named; the failure would have looked like an implementation bug · REQ-01 rework R1
- 2026-09-06 · With the modal open over a card, which surface acts on `Esc`? (REQ-REV-01 **B3**) · **The topmost open surface consumes Esc and no other surface acts on the same event**, stated inside the shared interface paragraph S02-R14 and S01-R20 carry byte-identically, with a hook on each side · Two slices, two lanes, zero precedent in the codebase to copy: two independent document-level keydown listeners would both fire on one Esc · REQ-01 rework R1, rule from REQ-REV-01 B3
- 2026-09-06 · Who writes the ONE shared modal-semantics helper? (REQ-REV-01 **P4**) · **S02 writes `apps/ui/components/consent/modalSemantics.ts`** — focus trap, initial focus, focus return, backdrop close, `prefers-reduced-motion`, Esc stack — and S01 consumes it unchanged. Added to S02's owned-file list, which v1 declared exhaustive while naming no helper at all; it is the mission's first artefact, ahead of the policy modal that consumes it · Ruled by the orchestrator in `COMMON.md` §10.7. Retrofitting the seven existing `aria-modal` overlays is ticket `A11Y-OVERLAYS` (`t_8962842f`), outside this mission · orchestrator (`COMMON.md` §10.7)
- 2026-09-06 · The set-equality assertion's line range in `tests/unit/t9-mode-tokens.test.ts` (REQ-REV-01 **N2**) · `:376-377`, not `:380-383`; `:379-384` is the separate raw VALUE loop. Corrected in S02-R22's source note · Measured; the substance (single-writer tokens) is unchanged · REQ-01 rework R1
- 2026-09-06 · **CONTESTED — the line of the manifest-equality assertion in `tests/unit/v2ui-node-runner.test.ts` (REQ-REV-01 N5)** · The citation stays `:19`; a note recording the re-measurement sits beside it so a later seat does not "fix" it back · The review says `:19` is a `.map(...)` and the assertion is at `:21`. **Measured by me in the main tree AND in lane `.worktrees/consent-s01/dialectical-engine`, identical md5 `dae7cd12705f1d7c885030ceefdbd433`, 35-line file: `:16` is the `.map(...)`, `:17` is `.sort();`, `:18` is blank, `:19` IS `expect(activeTests).toEqual([...manifest].sort());`, `:20` is `});`, `:21` is blank.** N5's substance — the constraint is real and a new `consent/*.source-test.mjs` must be registered — is accepted and unchanged · REQ-01 rework R1 (contesting REQ-REV-01 N5)
- 2026-09-06 · What "byte-exact from `design-data.js`" means, given the extract stores escapes (REQ-REV-01 **N8**) · Byte-exact is measured on the DECODED characters: a `\uXXXX` escape copied verbatim into `apps/ui/lib/privacyPolicy.ts` (a TypeScript module) decodes correctly; the same escape in a `.json` file or raw JSX text ships six visible characters to the reader. Stated at the end of §Copy and in S02-R20; the hooks assert `textContent`, never source bytes · ~20 escapes appear across `design-data.js:51,57-84`, and v1 left a correct decode to luck · REQ-01 rework R1
- 2026-09-06 · Where does the read-mode `Close` button's contract live? (REQ-REV-01 **N9**) · Inside the shared interface paragraph, so S01's own hook can pin it; S02's prose keeps the contact-line detail beside it · v1 stated it only on S02's side, so the consumer could not catch its absence · REQ-01 rework R1

### Rework round 2 — REQ-REV-01's round-2 verdict, applied (2026-09-06; `SPEC.md` is now v3, `SPEC-v2.md` is the archive)

- 2026-09-06 · How many idioms may a hook use to assert the submit button's state? (REQ-REV-01 **B4**) · **Exactly one: `field(name).click()` inside `await act(async () => { … })`.** The `new Event("change", { bubbles: true })` alternative is deleted from R17's hook, from §Tests and from `PLAN.md`; `new MouseEvent("click", { bubbles: true })` is measured-equivalent and is still NOT offered · Measured before the edit, `.hermes/reports/consent-ui/probes/b2-idiom-matrix.mjs`, React 19.2.8 / jsdom 30.0.1, three identical runs: `.click()` → DOM `true/true`, button enabled; `MouseEvent("click")` → `true/true`, enabled; `Event("change")` → `false/false`, **still disabled**; `Event("click")` → `false/false`, **still disabled**. React routes a checkbox's `onChange` through the click event and requires a real `MouseEvent`. Two idioms presented as equivalent is precisely what caused B4, so the count is one, not two · REQ-01 rework R2
- 2026-09-06 · **Supersedes the round-1 line above** — "How must a NEW test assert the submit button's enabled state?" · That line names `dispatchEvent(new Event("change", { bubbles: true }))` as a lawful alternative. **It is not; it is measured dead.** This file is append-only, so the round-1 line stays exactly as written and is read together with this one. Everything else in it — never assign `.checked` and then assert on the button; assignment stays correct for the three existing SUBMIT cases and for R18's refusal pins — is unchanged and still binding · A DECISIONS file that edits its own history stops being a record · REQ-01 rework R2
- 2026-09-06 · R17's fifth hook case was unsatisfiable under every idiom (REQ-REV-01 **B5**) · **The case now resets before it clicks:** assign `.checked = true` on both without dispatching → assert still `disabled`; **then set both back to `.checked = false`**, `click()` both, assert it enables. The toggle mechanism is stated in the sentence itself · `.click()` TOGGLES: from a pre-assigned `true` it drives the box to `false`, `onChange` fires with `false`, and the button correctly stays disabled — so v2's "then dispatch and assert it enables" could not pass against the CORRECT implementation, which is worse than a hook that is merely wrong. Measured: the reviewer's matrix (all four idioms fail from a pre-assigned `true`) and my own remedy probe (the reset passes, 3 runs) · REQ-01 rework R2
- 2026-09-06 · Does the rewritten case 5 keep the guarantee v2 attached to it — "a future seat that switches to controlled inputs breaks a test that names the reason"? · **No, measured — so the guarantee moved to a NEW case 6 that delivers it.** Case 6: assign `.checked = true` on `adult-affirmed` only without dispatching, `click()` `privacy-accepted` (which forces a React re-render), then assert the button is still `disabled` AND `field("adult-affirmed").checked` is still `true` · Measured, 4 implementation variants × 3 runs, deterministic: the rewritten case 5 is GREEN under uncontrolled+FormData, controlled+mirror, uncontrolled+mirror and controlled+FormData — it documents the toggle and detects nothing. Case 6 is RED under exactly the two CONTROLLED variants. An uncontrolled input keeps an assigned `.checked` across a re-render; a controlled input is re-rendered from React state and reset. Writing a hook whose stated purpose it cannot serve is the same defect class as B2/B4/B5 — an idiom prescribed without being executed · REQ-01 rework R2
- 2026-09-06 · Rejected alternatives for B5, recorded so nobody re-derives them (`COMMON.md` §10.1) · (a) **Splitting case 5 into two cases from fresh mounts** — measured to pass, but its second half becomes byte-identical to case 4 and it buys nothing while adding a case. (b) **Keeping `new MouseEvent("click", { bubbles: true })` as a documented alternative** — measured to work, rejected because B4's cause was two idioms offered as equivalent. (c) **Making case 6 assert only the button's `disabled` state** — measured green under all four variants, i.e. no discriminating power; the `.checked`-survives-a-re-render assertion is the load-bearing one · REQ-01 rework R2
- 2026-09-06 · How many `\uXXXX` escapes does `design-data.js` actually hold? (REQ-REV-01 **N11**) · **15, of three codepoints: `\u2014` ×12, `\u2019` ×2, `\u2192` ×1.** Counting rule and command now sit in §Copy beside the number: `grep -o '\\u[0-9A-Fa-f]\{4\}' design-data.js | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn`, run from `docs/missions/consent-ui/design/`. **U+00B7 is never escaped** (`grep -c -i 'u00b7'` → `0`); it is raw ×4, alongside raw U+2014 ×4 — so U+2014 occurs in BOTH forms and no character's form can be assumed · **Supersedes the round-1 line above** that says "~20 escapes appear across `design-data.js:51,57-84`": the number was an estimate and the line range was never the counting domain. Append-only, so that line stays as written · REQ-01 rework R2
- 2026-09-06 · v2's own N8 sentence promised the literal six-character escape and printed the decoded character (found while fixing N11) · §Copy now prints `\u2019` where it says escape and `’` where it says decoded · Measured: `SPEC-v2.md` contains the literal escape form **0** times and the raw U+2019 character 5 times, so the sentence demonstrated the opposite of what it asserted — on the one page whose whole subject is that the two forms differ · REQ-01 rework R2

### Architecture round — ARCH-S02 fills `PLAN.md` (2026-09-06; `SPEC.md` v3 is the frozen WHAT and was not touched)

**Standing residue first.**

- 2026-09-06 · **N12** — `SPEC.md:356` and `:692` state an absolute "never assign `.checked` then assert on the button", which R17's own cases 5 and 6 (`:373-385`) deliberately violate · **R17's case text governs; the six cases are planned exactly as R17 words them.** The absolute sentence is read as its own §Tests qualification already reads it: it governs assertions *about the BUTTON's enabled state reached by assignment alone*. Cases 5 and 6 assign and then assert on the button precisely to prove that the assignment did NOT move it — that is the same rule stated from the other side, not an exception to it. Case 6's second assertion (`field("adult-affirmed").checked` still `true`) is not about the button at all · The specific governs the general; a one-clause `SPEC-v4` is V's (row V-15 / REQ-REV-01's `V-CU-1` option (a)), and this line is option (b) — the PLAN-level clarification the reviewer named as the fallback, so no coding seat has to reconcile the two sentences. Steps `S02-S17`…`S02-S22` carry the six cases verbatim with this note attached · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Does the N12 ruling need V before coding starts? · **No.** The plan is written against R17's cases today; if V later authorizes `SPEC-v4`, the three clause edits change no step · The packet (§6) instructs "do not wait for it"; the plan is not blocked either way · ARCH-S02

**Sequencing and the cross-slice dependency.**

- 2026-09-06 · The cross-slice dependency runs both ways (S02 needs S01's tokens, S01 needs S02's helper and modal) — how is the deadlock broken? · **Measured: it is not a deadlock. `apps/ui/components/consent/modalSemantics.ts` is pure TypeScript/DOM behaviour and references no CSS token, and `apps/ui/lib/privacyPolicy.ts` is pure data. Both are buildable and fully verifiable at base `2b670d30` with no merge.** Every S02 cluster is therefore unblocked at base; only the *browser* appearance of S02's CSS waits on S01. `git merge slice/consent-s01` is ONE explicit step, placed LAST (`S02-S45`, cluster `S02-C9`), and it is performed by the orchestrator, never by a coding seat · S02's CSS references `var(--scrim)` / `var(--z-policy-*)` / `var(--ok-edge)` before S01 declares them; no test fails on an unresolved custom property — `tests/unit/t9-mode-tokens.test.ts` asserts set equality over *declarations* (`:376-377`) and the colour-literal gate (`:541-561`) scans for `#hex`/`rgb(`/`oklch(` literals, and a `var()` reference is neither. Verified by reading both assertions · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Which artefact is `S02-C1`? · **`modalSemantics.ts` alone**, with its own two-surface Esc pin — not the three-file bundle the orchestrator's hint suggested · Three reasons, in order: (1) it is the artefact S01 is *blocked* on (`S01-R18`, the Esc stack), so shipping it first converts a two-way dependency into a one-way one; (2) it needs no token, so it needs no merge; (3) REQ-REV-01 r3 prediction 2 names the Esc stack across two surfaces as the mission's next defect — "both SPECs specify it, both pin it in jsdom, and **nobody has ever run it**" — so it earns the smallest, earliest, most-probed cluster in the slice · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Measured correction to `SPEC.md` R22's "the five tokens it consumes … are declared by S01 (S01-R24)" · **Four of the five are new; `--ok-dot` already exists.** `grep -c -- '--ok-dot:' apps/ui/app/globals.css` → `2` (both blocks), and `--ok-dot` is not among S01-R24's ten. `--ok-edge`, `--scrim`, `--z-policy-scrim`, `--z-policy-card` → `0` each, and all four are in S01-R24's table · Recorded, not repaired: `SPEC.md` is frozen and the substance (S02 declares nothing, S01 is the sole writer) is unaffected. It matters only because it shortens what S02 actually waits on · ARCH-S02

**Markup and DOM shape.**

- 2026-09-06 · Where is the policy modal rendered from, given the house pattern has no portal? · **Inline, as a sibling immediately AFTER `</form>` inside `AuthShell`'s children** — no `createPortal` · Two independent reasons. (1) Correctness: a `<button>` inside the sign-up `<form>` is a submit control by default and `Enter` inside the modal could submit the registration; rendering outside the form removes the hazard entirely rather than relying on `type="button"` discipline. (2) House vocabulary (`COMMON.md` §9): `createPortal` has 0 hits under `apps/ui`, and all seven existing overlays render inline. **Measured that the fixed-position scrim escapes its ancestors:** none of `.appShell` (`globals.css:298-303`), `.authScreen` (`:709-718`), `.authCard` (`:721-730`), `.authCardInner` (`:733-737`), `.authPanel` (`:786-793`), `.authPanelCore` (`:794-799`) or `.authForm` (`:801-808`) declares `transform`, `filter`, `perspective`, `will-change` or `contain`, so none becomes the containing block for a `position: fixed` descendant, and `overflow: hidden` on `.authCard:725` does not clip a descendant whose containing block is the viewport. `--z-policy-scrim: 77` clears `.topBar`'s `z-index: 30` (`:318`) · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · What keeps that measurement true after a later CSS edit? · **Step `S02-S42` adds a source-text assertion** in `tests/unit/consent-s02-style-contract.test.ts`: every rule whose selector text contains one of those seven class names declares none of `transform:`, `filter:`, `perspective:`, `will-change:`, `contain:` · The alternative is an assumption nobody can re-check; this converts a browser-only property into a mechanical one, and it fails loudly the day someone animates the auth card · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · **Pre-authorized fallback, so it does not cost a rework round:** if V's acceptance step 4 or 5 shows the modal clipped, mispositioned or behind the card · The remedy is `createPortal(…, document.body)` inside `PrivacyPolicyModal.tsx` only, with no other change; it is authorized in advance and needs no new decision · The inline placement is an inference from CSS text, not a browser observation, and the whole cost of being wrong is one import · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Row markup, given the design draws two `<div>` rows and one row contains a link · **Row 1 (18+) is a `<label className="consentRow">` wrapping its input and its text; row 2 (privacy) is a `<div className="consentRow">` whose input carries `aria-labelledby` pointing at the text span's id.** · HTML's content model for `<label>` forbids descendant *interactive content*, and row 2's `Privacy Policy` control is interactive — so row 2 cannot be a `<label>` without producing invalid markup. Row 1 has no interactive descendant, so the native label gives R04 ("the square, the text or the row toggles it") for free with zero JavaScript. `aria-labelledby` gives row 2 the same accessible name mechanically, and jsdom can assert it · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Is `Privacy Policy` an `<a>` or a `<button>`? · **`<button type="button" className="consentPolicyLink">`** · It opens a modal; it navigates nowhere. **Rejected:** `<a href="#10c">` (the design's literal markup) — it would push a history entry or jump the page and then need `preventDefault` on a target that does not exist; and `<a>` with no `href` is not keyboard-focusable, which would break `SPEC.md` §Accessibility ("reachable by Tab and activates the modal with `Enter`"). `type="button"` is load-bearing: a bare `<button>` inside a form defaults to `type="submit"` · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · The 17px check square: styled sibling, or the input itself? · **The input itself**, `appearance: none`, 17×17, `border-radius: 5px`, `1.5px solid var(--ok-edge)`, and `:checked` → `background: var(--ok-dot)` with the `✓` glyph from a `::after` pseudo-element in `var(--core)` at 10px/800 · REQ-01 ruled "a styled sibling **or pseudo-element**, rather than replacing the input"; this is the pseudo-element branch and it is the smaller of the two — one element keeps native focus, native `Space`, native `required` and one focus ring. **Rejected:** a visually-hidden input plus a sibling `<span>` square — it needs sibling/`:has()` selectors for every state and duplicates the focus ring onto an element that is not the control · ARCH-S02

**Behaviour.**

- 2026-09-06 · The single click rule for the privacy row (R05 + R06 in one handler) · **One `onClick` on the row: if the mirror says the box is CHECKED → do not `preventDefault`; if the click did not originate on the input, call `input.click()` so the box unchecks and the mirror updates; return, opening nothing. If it is UNCHECKED → `event.preventDefault()` unconditionally, then open the modal.** · `preventDefault` on the row covers all three entry points at once: on the input it cancels the native toggle, on the button it cancels nothing harmful, on the span there is nothing to cancel. Routing the checked-branch through `input.click()` rather than an assignment keeps the DOM and the R17 mirror in step through the one path React actually listens to · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Does clicking the `Privacy Policy` control while the box is CHECKED untick it, or reopen the policy? · **It unticks it, exactly like the rest of the row** · R06 and the states table both scope the behaviour to "the privacy row", and R05 is the requirement that enumerates entry points — it enumerates them for the UNCHECKED case only. One uniform rule beats a special case that neither requirement states. **STRONGEST COUNTER:** a reader who has accepted may want to re-read the policy and will be surprised to untick it instead; if V dislikes it, the remedy is one `if` in the same handler. Flagged in the handoff as a V-visible behaviour, CONFIDENCE medium · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · **CONTESTED, routed up with a recommendation** — `Space` on the focused `privacy-accepted` input dispatches a real `click` on that input, so it takes R05's path: from unchecked it opens the modal instead of ticking. `SPEC.md` R03 says both inputs are "toggled by `Space`" · **Recommendation: the R05/R06 path governs (specific over general), and R03's `Space` clause is read as satisfied for `adult-affirmed` in both directions and for `privacy-accepted` in the checked→unchecked direction.** The alternative — letting `Space` tick the privacy box without the modal — would create a keyboard-only route around the read gate, which is the one thing V's goal forbids · Same shape as N12. Not repaired in the frozen SPEC; the plan pins the behaviour (`S02-S33`) and V confirms at acceptance step 14. CONFIDENCE high that the modal path is what V wants; the defect is only that R03's sentence is now imprecise · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · `I have read it` must both tick the box AND enable `Create account` (V acceptance step 8), yet a programmatic `.checked = true` fires no React change · **`onAcknowledge` sets BOTH: `privacyInputRef.current.checked = true` and `setPrivacyMirror(true)`, in that order, in the same handler.** · This is the B2/B4 class arriving from a new direction: the component is doing to itself exactly what the dead test idioms do. Setting only the DOM leaves the button disabled; setting only the mirror leaves `FormData` empty and R18 refuses the registration. **`S02-S36` pins both halves in one case** — acknowledge, then assert `field("privacy-accepted").checked === true` AND, after `field("adult-affirmed").click()`, that the submit button is not disabled · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · The Esc stack's implementation shape · **A module-level LIFO array in `modalSemantics.ts` plus exactly ONE `document` `keydown` listener, installed when the array becomes non-empty and removed when it empties.** On `Escape`, only the LAST entry's `onClose` is invoked, and the handler calls `event.preventDefault()` and `event.stopPropagation()`. `Tab` is handled on the topmost entry only · A per-surface listener cannot know it is topmost; a stack with two listeners passes every other hook in the SPEC and fails only the direct two-surface pin (`SPEC.md` R16), which is why that pin is `S02-S05` and is the first assertion of the first cluster. **Rejected:** a React context provider — it would force S01's card and S02's modal into one tree, and they are mounted from different components in different slices · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · The focus trap's focusable set · **`a[href], button:not([disabled]), input:not([disabled]), select:not([disabled]), textarea:not([disabled]), [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])`, queried inside the surface at the moment `Tab` is pressed, never cached** · The set changes while the modal is open — `I have read it` is `disabled` until the scroll gate latches, so a cached list would trap focus onto a control that has since become reachable, or wrap to one that is now disabled. Querying live costs one `querySelectorAll` per `Tab` · ARCH-S02

**Measured jsdom facts that change the implementation (all three would have cost a coding round).**

- 2026-09-06 · `window.matchMedia` in the test environment · **It does not exist. Measured in the lane, jsdom 30.0.1: `typeof window.matchMedia` → `undefined`.** So `modalSemantics.ts` exports `prefersReducedMotion()` guarded as `typeof window !== "undefined" && typeof window.matchMedia === "function" ? window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches : false`, and the test stubs `window.matchMedia` when it wants the `true` branch · An unguarded call throws in EVERY render test that mounts the modal, and the failure names `matchMedia`, not the seat's design. Probe: `scratchpad/arch-consent-s02/env-probe.mjs` · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · `Element.prototype.scrollIntoView` in the test environment · **It does not exist either. Measured, same probe: `typeof el.scrollIntoView` → `undefined`.** So the jump-pill handler calls it optionally — `target?.scrollIntoView?.({ block: "start", behavior: prefersReducedMotion() ? "auto" : "smooth" })` — and `S02-S28` stubs it per target element to assert WHICH target was resolved and WHICH behaviour was passed · One `?.` removes a whole class of jsdom breakage, and stubbing turns `SPEC.md` R11's "jsdom pins the mapping" into a strictly stronger pin (the handler's resolution) without ever claiming jsdom scrolled anything · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Can a jsdom test drive the scroll-gate arithmetic? · **Yes. Measured: `Object.defineProperty(el, "scrollHeight"|"clientHeight"|"scrollTop", { value, configurable: true, writable: true })` shadows the prototype getters and can be redefined between assertions.** Same probe: `scrollTop=292, clientHeight=200, scrollHeight=500` → reached `true`; `scrollTop=291` → `false`. `configurable: true` is load-bearing — without it the latch case cannot scroll back up · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · The disabled `I have read it` contrast, left UNVERIFIED by REQ-01 and by all three review rounds · **`opacity: .65`.** Derivation, executed not asserted: WCAG 2.2 ratio of the composited label over the composited button face on the `--shell` footer ground, both modes, `opacity` stepped by 0.05 — `.60` → Terracotta **4.13** (fails 4.5), `.65` → Terracotta **4.79** / Chamber **7.17**, `.70` → 5.54 / 8.09. `.65` is the smallest 0.05 step that clears 4.5:1 in BOTH modes, and it is the most-dimmed (most legibly "disabled") value that does. Enabled state measures 14.00 / 15.72 · `tests/support/contrast.ts` takes `#RRGGBB` only, so `S02-S43` composites `α·fg + (1-α)·bg` per channel in the test and passes the flattened hexes to `contrastRatio` — the number's derivation is the test. Probe output pasted in `PLAN.md` §Clusters · ARCH-S02

**Verification mechanics.**

- 2026-09-06 · **A NEW variant of the acceptance-command family, measured here** — `pnpm exec vitest run <existing> <missing>` runs only the existing file, prints `Tests 17 passed (17)`, and **exits 0**. A cluster command naming three files, one of which was never created, reports GREEN having never executed it · **Every S02 cluster command carries a third arm: an anchored `Test Files <n> passed (<n>)` assertion with `<n>` the exact number of files listed.** Validated on known-GOOD and known-BAD input per `TOOLING-TRAPS.md`: 1 real file/expect 1 → VERDICT 0; 2 real/expect 2 → VERDICT 0; 1 real + 1 missing/expect 2 → VERDICT 1 (caught); a RED suite/expect 1 → VERDICT 1 · Variants 1-6 of this family are already in `TOOLING-TRAPS.md`; this is the seventh and it is the mirror of variant 1 — the command cannot see part of its target and says nothing. Appended to the traps file · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Where do the two inherited-RED gates (`t9-mode-tokens`, `pnpm typecheck`) live, given the packet asks for them "in every cluster's verification command"? · **In a `## Standing gates` block that every cluster runs and reports, NOT inside the cluster's ONE guard.** · The lawful guard asserts a GREEN summary line with no `failed`; both gates are RED at base *by design* (`BASELINE.md`), so folding them in would make every cluster command permanently red and unable to discriminate anything. `BASELINE.md`'s own rule is "assert the DELTA, never the absolute", and a delta is a different assertion, not the same one. Both gates are classified under charge A9 beside the cluster commands, with their base output pasted · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Does S02 add a `consent/*.source-test.mjs`? · **No, and therefore `apps/ui/scripts/node-test-manifest.json` is not edited.** The CSS and source-text pins live in `tests/unit/consent-s02-style-contract.test.ts`, a vitest file that reads `globals.css` with `readFileSync` · Precedent: `tests/unit/t9-mode-tokens.test.ts:35,551` does exactly this. Adding a `.mjs` suite would force a same-change edit to the manifest (`tests/unit/v2ui-node-runner.test.ts:19` asserts exact set equality over a recursive scan of `apps/ui`) for no gain. **If a later round does add one, the manifest edit is mandatory and its `apps/ui`-relative path is the entry** · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Where does the pure-data test live, given the PLAN scaffold's allowed list says "new tests under `tests/render/`"? · **`tests/unit/consent-privacy-policy-data.test.ts`** · `COMMON.md` §3 grants this mission "`tests/render/**` + `tests/unit/**` for the pins you add"; the scaffold's phrase is the common case, not an exclusion (the same list also conditionally allows a file under `apps/ui/scripts/`). The module under test imports nothing from the DOM, so a `.tsx` under `tests/render/` would advertise a jsdom environment it does not use · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Does every cluster that needs CSS append to `globals.css`? · **No — ALL of S02's CSS lands in ONE cluster, `S02-C8`, which is the only cluster with `globals.css` in its file surface.** · The one file both slices write is `globals.css`; putting it in one cluster's surface makes the single-writer story exact, keeps the merge-conflict window to one commit, and lets `S02-C8`'s command own every source-text pin (R22 no colour literal, R24 reduced motion, R09 geometry). **Rejected:** distributing CSS per rendering cluster — every jsdom test in this slice computes no layout, so nothing is verified earlier by styling earlier, and it would put the shared file in four clusters' `allowed` lists · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · Why are `S02-C3` (group) and `S02-C4` (gate) separate when both edit `SignUpFlow.tsx`? · **Because they have different RED-first oracles.** `S02-C3`'s added line `field("privacy-accepted").checked = true;` in the three existing cases is RED because the INPUT does not exist (`field()` throws on a null lookup, `auth-flow-integration.test.tsx:35-39`) and GREEN when it does. `S02-C4`'s two new refusal pins are RED because the HANDLER does not yet refuse, and GREEN when it does. Merging them would hide one failure behind the other · `S02-C3` deliberately asserts **nothing** about the privacy row's click behaviour or the submit button, so no `S02-C3` assertion is rewritten by a later cluster · ARCH-S02
- 2026-09-06 · An ADR? · **Warranted, and NOT written here** — proposed path `docs/architecture/01-decisions/ADR-0NN-shared-modal-semantics.md`, to be authored after the slice lands · `modalSemantics.ts` is a new repo-wide boundary that outlives this mission: ticket `A11Y-OVERLAYS` (`t_8962842f`) will retrofit seven existing overlays onto it, and the Esc-stack rule ("the topmost open surface consumes Esc and no other surface acts on the same event") is law for every future overlay, not for these two. Creating the file is outside this seat's `allowed` list, so this line is the record and the orchestrator routes the ticket · ARCH-S02

### Rework round 1 — ARCH-REV-S02-r1's verdict, applied (2026-09-06; `SPEC.md` v3 still frozen and untouched, md5 `ad060bda81db71f00f4c70b1dbf63f2f`)

**Seat: ARCH-S02-REWORK-R1, fresh session, round 1 of max 3. This file is append-only: not one
